the promisor acted consistently with the alleged\ncommitment\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: Statements that are merely indefinite or that do\nnot identify the promised performance may not initially be\nenforceable\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n                           PAGE BREAK\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\n[0m[1mRule: Restatement of Contracts § 32, Comment c — indefiniteness[0m\n[0m[1mmay be cured by later specification[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Restatement of Contracts § 32, Comment c, as applied\nin Greiner v. Greiner\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: Greiner v Greiner.pdf\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: A promise that is initially too indefinite because\nno particular land was specified may become definite when the\npromisor later segregates specific property for the promisee,\nprepares it for the promised use, and gives the promisee\npossession\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: Whether later conduct made a previously\nindefinite promise sufficiently definite to enforce?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n1. The original promise is sufficiently manifested to identify\nthe general subject of the promised performance\n2. The promisor later identifies or segregates the specific\nproperty or performance\n3. The promisor acts consistently with the promise by preparing\nthe property or performance for the promisee\n4. The promisee receives possession or otherwise obtains the\npromised benefit\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: The court first treated Maggie’s promise of “a\nhome” or “a little land for a home” as indefinite because no\nparticular tract was identified, then relied on the later\ndesignation of the 80-acre tract, relocation of the buildings,\npreparation of the house, and delivery of possession\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: The promise was unenforceable at the initial stage\nbecause the land was indefinite, but the court held that later\nconduct removed that indefiniteness\n\n[0m[1mRule: Restatement of Contracts § 90 — promise reasonably[0m\n[0m[1minducing definite and substantial action is binding[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Restatement of the Law of Contracts § 90, quoted and\napplied in Greiner v. Greiner\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: Greiner v Greiner.pdf\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: A promise that the promisor should reasonably\nexpect to induce action or forbearance of a definite and\nsubstantial character, and that does induce such action or\nforbearance, is binding if injustice can be avoided only by\nenforcing the promise\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n                           PAGE BREAK\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: Whether Frank’s definite and substantial\nreliance on Maggie’s promise made the promise binding even\nthough traditional consideration was disputed?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n1. The promisor should reasonably expect the promise to induce\naction or forbearance\n2. The promise induces action or forbearance of a definite and\nsubstantial character\n3. Enforcement is necessary to avoid injustice\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: Frank gave up his Logan County homestead, moved\nto Mitchell County, established himself and his family on the\n80-acre tract, made lasting and valuable improvements, incurred\nother expenditures, and lived there for nearly a year before\nreceiving notice to quit\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: The promise is not automatically enforceable\nmerely because action occurred; the action must be definite and\nsubstantial, and enforcement must be necessary to avoid\ninjustice\n\n[0m[1mRule: Promissory reliance may supply the basis for enforcement[0m\n[0m[1mdespite the promisor’s claim that the promisee did nothing for[0m\n[0m[1mthe promisor[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Application of Restatement of the Law of Contracts §\n90 in Greiner v. Greiner\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: Greiner v Greiner.pdf\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: When a promise reasonably induces definite and\nsubstantial reliance, the promise may be binding even though the\npromisor argues that the promisee furnished no traditional\nconsideration\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: Whether enforcement is available when the\npromisee’s conduct benefited the promisee more directly than the\npromisor?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m: The promisee must have taken definite and\nsubstantial action in reliance on the promise, and refusing\nenforcement must create an injustice that cannot adequately be\navoided through money relief\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: Maggie argued that she had done everything for\nFrank and Frank had done nothing for her, but the court treated\nFrank’s relocation, abandonment of his homestead, improvements,\nexpenditures, and establishment of a family home as sufficient\nreliance\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: The court did not hold that every family promise\nor gift promise is enforceable; its decision depended on the\nextent of Frank’s reliance and the inadequacy of a money\njudgment\n\n[0m[1mCase Narrative[0m\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n                           PAGE BREAK\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n\nMaggie Greiner was the widow of Peter Greiner and the plaintiff\nin the lawsuit. Frank Greiner was her son and the defendant.\nPeter died testate and left Maggie a widow with several sons and\ndaughters. Four children—Henry, Frank, Nicholas, and Kate—had\nbeen disinherited and received only five dollars each under\nPeter’s will. Henry later died unmarried and intestate in June\n1925, and Maggie inherited considerable property from him. She\nthen decided to place Frank and Nicholas on an equal footing\nwith the children who had been favored in Peter’s will.\nMaggie initially intended to give Frank and Nicholas land,\napproximately 90 acres to each. She later entered into a written\ncontract to pay Nicholas \$2,000. Frank had moved to Logan\nCounty, homesteaded a quarter section, and lived there for\nsixteen or seventeen years. Maggie lived in Mitchell County,\nwhere the land involved in the dispute was located near her\nhome. The opinion notes a dispute over whether Maggie inherited\na three-sevenths interest or the entire interest in the 80-acre\ntract, although Maggie testified that she inherited the entire\ninterest.\nIn July 1926, Maggie had Nicholas write to Frank and ask him to\ncome to Mitchell County because she intended to make a\nsettlement with Frank and Nicholas. Frank came to Mitchell\nCounty and spoke with Maggie. There was a house on a quarter\nsection at the time. Maggie told Frank she was going to pay him\nand Nicholas. Frank responded that he did not want money; he\nwanted a home and a little land for a home. Maggie said that she\nhad the land, told him to move into the house, and said they\nwould divide things later. Frank agreed and said he would move\nback.\nFrank initially encountered an obstacle to moving. Louis\nGreiner, one of the children favored under Peter’s will,\ntestified that Maggie wanted Frank to move onto the 80 acres\nbecause Frank had been disinherited and she wanted to equalize\nthe treatment of the two disinherited sons. Frank had a mortgage\non horses and mules and could not move immediately. Maggie gave\nLouis money to pay off the mortgage. Louis paid it, the mortgage\nwas assigned to Maggie, and Frank subsequently repaid Maggie.\nAfter the mortgage was released, Frank and his family moved\nback.\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n                           PAGE BREAK\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n\nOther family members heard Maggie describe the arrangement as\nFrank’s share of the property. A bank cashier who helped prepare\nthe written contract between Maggie and Nicholas testified that\nMaggie said she was bringing Frank back to Mitchell County\nbecause he was not doing well where he had been living and that\nshe intended to give him an interest in the land. Louis\ntestified that by the fall of 1926 it was a settled fact that\nFrank would move onto the 80 acres and that Maggie was giving\nhim that place as his share. Albert Greiner heard Maggie say she\nwas going to move the buildings onto the 80 acres for Frank and\nwas going to settle with Frank and Nicholas because they had\nbeen disinherited. Flora Greiner testified that Maggie said\nFrank was moving back, that she was going to give him the 80\nacres, and that the buildings would be moved there so Frank\ncould have it as a home.\nFrank moved back on September 20, 1926. Maggie then decided to\nmove the house and other buildings from the quarter section onto\nthe 80-acre tract. Frank testified that Maggie told him the\nhouse and buildings would be moved there and that the tract\nwould be a home for him. The buildings were moved, Maggie fixed\nthe house and otherwise prepared the property for Frank’s\noccupancy, and Frank began occupying the 80 acres in the spring\nof 1927. Maggie herself testified that Frank moved onto the\nproperty after she had arranged and fixed the house and that he\nhad lived there ever since.\nThe parties still needed to determine how Frank’s title would be\nassured. They first contemplated a will. Louis testified that\nMaggie had planned to make a will in favor of Frank and Nicholas\nbut abandoned that plan after another son, August “Gustie”\nGreiner, threatened that she would be moved off the property if\nshe made a will. Maggie then said she would let Frank have the\nproperty as it was without making a will. Later, Maggie said she\nwould give Frank a deed, but no deed was executed. August lived\nwith Maggie, had returned from California shortly after Maggie\nmade the written contract to pay Nicholas \$2,000, and had fought\nwith Frank and Albert. Although August helped move the house, he\ntestified that he never heard that Maggie intended to give Frank\nthe 80 acres.\nMaggie eventually brought a forcible-detention action against\nFrank to recover possession of a quarter section and an\nadditional 80-acre tract. Frank responded that Maggie had given\nhim the 80 acres under circumstances that prevented her from\nreclaiming it and that she should execute a deed to him. The\ndispute therefore arose because Maggie had not delivered the\ndeed and sought to recover possession after Frank had relocated,\nestablished his family on the property, and made expenditures\nand improvements in reliance on the arrangement.\n[0m[1mCase Brief: Greiner v. Greiner[0m\n[0m[1mCase Name: Greiner v. Greiner, Supreme Court of Kansas, December[0m\n[0m[1m6, 1930, Burch, J.[0m\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n                           PAGE BREAK\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: Greiner v Greiner.pdf\n- [0m[1mCourse[0m: Contracts — Promissory Estoppel\n- [0m[1mFacts[0m\n- Maggie Greiner, the plaintiff, was Frank Greiner’s mother\n- Frank Greiner, the defendant, was one of the children\ndisinherited under Peter Greiner’s will and received only five\ndollars\n- Maggie inherited considerable property from her son Henry\nafter Henry died unmarried and intestate in June 1925\n- Maggie decided to equalize the treatment of Frank and Nicholas\nwith the children favored under Peter’s will\n- Maggie initially contemplated giving Frank and Nicholas\napproximately 90 acres each, but later made a written contract\nto pay Nicholas \$2,000\n- Frank had lived in Logan County for sixteen or seventeen\nyears, where he homesteaded a quarter section\n- Maggie lived in Mitchell County, and the land at issue was in\nMitchell County near her home\n- In July 1926, Maggie sent for Frank through Nicholas so she\ncould make a settlement with Frank and Nicholas\n- Frank rejected money and requested a home and a little land\nfor a home\n- Maggie said she had the land, told Frank to move into the\nhouse, and said they would divide the property later\n- Frank agreed to move back to Mitchell County\n- Frank could not initially move because he had a mortgage on\nhorses and mules\n- Maggie supplied Louis Greiner with money to pay off the\nmortgage, allowing Frank to move\n- The mortgage was assigned to Maggie, and Frank later repaid\nher\n- Frank moved back to Mitchell County on September 20, 1926\n- Maggie later decided that the house and buildings on the\nquarter section should be moved to the 80-acre tract for Frank\n- Frank testified that Maggie said the relocated house and\nbuildings would provide a home for him\n- Family members testified that Maggie repeatedly described the\n80 acres as Frank’s share or as land she was giving him because\nhe had been disinherited\n- The 80-acre tract was segregated for Frank, the buildings were\nmoved onto it, and Maggie prepared the house for his occupancy\n- Frank began occupying the 80 acres in the spring of 1927\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n                           PAGE BREAK\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n- Frank established himself and his family on the tract, made\nlasting and valuable improvements, and incurred other\nexpenditures\n- Frank lived on the property for nearly a year before receiving\nnotice to quit\n- Maggie contemplated assuring Frank’s title through a will,\nlater said she would let him have the property without a will,\nand eventually said she would give him a deed\n- No deed was executed\n- Maggie later sought to recover possession of the land\n- [0m[1mProcedural Posture[0m\n- Mitchell County District Court: ordered Plaintiff Maggie\nGreiner to execute a deed conveying the 80-acre tract to\nDefendant Frank Greiner on Maggie’s forcible-detention action\nand Frank’s claim for affirmative relief\n- Appeal of right: Plaintiff-Appellant Maggie Greiner sought\nreview in the Supreme Court of Kansas on the